sort of like this,<p><pre><code>    #include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

    static int generator(int st, float *out) {
        switch (st++) {
            case 0: *out = 1.0f; return st;
            case 1: *out = 7.0f; return st;
            case 2: *out = 9.0f; return st;
        }
        return 0;
    }

    int main(void) {
        float f;
        for (int st = 0; (st = generator(st, &amp;f));) {
            printf(&quot;generator yielded %g\n&quot;, (double)f);
        }
        return 0;
    }
</code></pre>
but notice you also have the option to make the state static rather than a parameter, which gives you more encapsulation of your state machine at the cost of being way less flexible in terms of threading and reentrance.

That doesn&#x27;t stop the C++ committee from doing the obvious, trivial and correct thing.<p><pre><code>  Point foo = {
     .y = foo(),
     .x = bar(),
  };
</code></pre>
calls foo(), sets x to it, calls bar(), and sets y to it. And<p><pre><code>  Point foo = {
     .x = bar(),
     .y = foo(),
  };
</code></pre>
calls bar(), sets x to it, then calls foo(), and sets y to it.<p>I&#x27;m sure you can give me the reason they didn&#x27;t do that, and it will be something like &quot;this conflicts with LRR-value warp semantics when invoked from concept inside a consteval coroutine&quot; or something equally stupid, but the problem is I don&#x27;t care. This is a good piece of syntax sugar (and the only reason I use C++20) and it&#x27;s being handicapped by insane features that I don&#x27;t use and don&#x27;t care about.

I was aware of this but I thought it didn&#x27;t work if you had any inline default initialisers i.e.<p><pre><code>   struct FooParams {
     int x = getX();
     const char* y = getY();
   };
</code></pre>
It turns out this was an issue all the way back in C++11, which didn&#x27;t even have designated initialisers, but inline initialisers like this prevented the struct from being an aggregate, which meant you couldn&#x27;t write FooParams{1, &quot;y&quot;}. That was fixed in C++14 (as I&#x27;ve just discovered).

C++&#x27;s version of this is more verbose but still sometimes worth it: make the caller instantiate the struct and initialise it as separate statements:<p><pre><code>   FooParams fooParams;  &#x2F;&#x2F; Gives sensible defaults to all members
   fooParams.x = 1;
   fooParams.y = &quot;xyz&quot;;
   foo(fooParams);
</code></pre>
It doesn&#x27;t work so well if you want to move construct things because you need to move at least twice: once when moving the value into the member, then you need to move the whole struct to the function argument `foo(std::move(fooParams))`. If you want it to copy something but avoid unnecessary copying then it&#x27;s even more annoying because you need to move to the function argument still which looks odd when your goal is to copy overall.

Tbh I would skip all the preprocessor magic proposed in the article and just go for plain C99 &#x27;option bag struct&#x27; arguments, the called function only needs to be aware that &quot;zero means default&quot;.<p>I&#x27;ve been using this style for almost 10 years now in the sokol headers, and it <i>still</i> feels like the right thing to do and actually more ergonomic than most other languages for initializing complex structs, e.g. see here for creating a 3D render pipeline object with dozens of creation options, but most of them keeping their default values:<p><pre><code>    state.pip = sg_make_pipeline(&amp;(sg_pipeline_desc){
        .layout = {
            .attrs = {
                [ATTR_texcube_pos].format = SG_VERTEXFORMAT_FLOAT3,
                [ATTR_texcube_color0].format = SG_VERTEXFORMAT_UBYTE4N,
                [ATTR_texcube_texcoord0].format = SG_VERTEXFORMAT_SHORT2N
            }
        },
        .shader = shd,
        .index_type = SG_INDEXTYPE_UINT16,
        .cull_mode = SG_CULLMODE_BACK,
        .depth = {
            .compare = SG_COMPAREFUNC_LESS_EQUAL,
            .write_enabled = true
        },
        .label = &quot;texcube-pipeline&quot;
    });
</code></pre>
(or see here: <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;floooh&#x2F;sokol-samples&#x2F;blob&#x2F;11bbe1a6865e93cea3ed5cbcd28a32f0794707d5&#x2F;sapp&#x2F;texcube-sapp.c#L124-L140" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;floooh&#x2F;sokol-samples&#x2F;blob&#x2F;11bbe1a6865e93c...</a>)<p>...this code snippet also makes use of C99 features that are not available in C++20 though (or most other languages), and it&#x27;s really remarkable how <i>well</i> designed the C99 designated init feature as a whole is (even though it was an afterthought) and how badly its individual subfeatures were understood by most later languages (basically, only Odin got it &quot;mostly right&quot;: <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;floooh&#x2F;sokol-odin&#x2F;blob&#x2F;7d47a31cad83a26ba1322a276336a7838c196cf5&#x2F;examples&#x2F;texcube&#x2F;main.odin#L113-L128" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;floooh&#x2F;sokol-odin&#x2F;blob&#x2F;7d47a31cad83a26ba1...</a>)

The idiom I usually use is to define a DEFAULT_FOO_PARAMS like<p><pre><code>  struct foo { int Z; int A; double E, double v }; 
  #define DEFAULT_FOO_PARAMS  .Z = 1, v = 3e8 

  foo_fn ( &amp;(struct foo){ DEFAULT_FOO_PARAMS, .Z  =2, .A = 4, .E = 1 });

</code></pre>
But I do sort of wish there was a language-supported way to set defaults for a designated initializer in the struct definition (like C++ default values) to avoid having to write DEFAULT_FOO_PARAMS there.
